From 8efda7b417c0c477e00de65811107a09bc3dc5dd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bemly <bemly_@petalmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 00:29:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Sinytra patch 0003: stream the response body (no base64, no
 16MB cap)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

0001's base64+cap rested on 'the channel is in the child, Java streams
cannot cross the JVM' — 0002's topology finding invalidated that: the
interception query runs entirely in the app process (parent-side real
nsHttpChannel), so the body crosses a JNI boundary, not a process one.

- GeckoViewResponseStreams (new, @WrapForJNI): process-level registry;
  the query handler registers a GeckoViewInputStream wrapping the app's
  InputStream and ships the id in the bundle; native takes it by id.
- responseToBundle: drain/base64/RESPONSE_BODY_MAX_BYTES removed —
  bodyStreamId only (absent = empty body).
- OnSuccess/Synthesize: id -> GeckoViewResponseStreams::Take ->
  ResponseBodyStream (subclass exposing the protected JNI-ref ctor) ->
  StartSynthesizedResponse; incremental reads over JNI, no cap —
  Chromium streams shouldInterceptRequest bodies the same way.
- widget/android/moz.build: register the new class in
  classes_with_WrapForJNI (alphabetical).
- GeckoViewResponseController.h: include the full
  ServiceWorkerInterceptController definition — StaticComponents.cpp
  instantiates the inline ctor/dtor (RefPtr member) and a forward
  declaration only worked through fragile unified-bundle include luck;
  registering the new class shifted the bundles and it broke.

Build-order note: new GeneratedJNI headers need 'mach build export'
before 'mach build binaries' (binaries skips the export tier).
